course: dictionary
lesson: 13535
title: Reading between the lines
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An implication is something that is suggested but not said directly. Writers often leave hints for the reader to pick up. «Tom looked at the empty biscuit tin and said nothing.» The writer never says Tom is cross, but the silence and the empty tin imply that someone has eaten his biscuits.
explain: An implication can also be a result that something will have later. «If it rains all week, the match will be cancelled.» The implication is that there will be no football. Ask yourself: if this is true, what follows from it?
explain: In formal writing you may read «What are the implications of this result?» This means «What will it lead to?» Scientists also talk about the implications of pollution for animals, meaning what pollution will do to them. Check the words around the word, as always, to be sure which meaning fits.
fact: An implication is a possible result or effect that something will have later.
fact: An implication is something that is suggested but not said directly.
fact: «Implication» can also mean being shown to be part of something bad, as in his implication in the crime.
fact: The phrase «by implication» means something is suggested without being stated outright.
fact: «Serious implications» means results that could be important or harmful.
exercise: «The lights were off and the door was locked.» What is the implication? | The shop has just opened | *The shop is closed for the day | The shop is having a big party
exercise: «If the bridge is washed away, the buses cannot cross.» What is one implication of this? | *Buses cannot get across the river | The bridge is brand new | Buses will be faster
exercise: In «What are the implications of this result?», what does implications mean? | Things hidden inside a box | *What it will lead to | Sounds made by a crowd | Names of the people who won
test: «Mum did not say a word, but she pointed at the muddy footprints on the carpet.» What is the implication? | *Someone has walked in with dirty shoes | Mum has bought a new carpet | Mum is going out for a walk | The footprints are very pretty
test: Dad says, «Think about the implications of losing for next season.» What does he mean? | The team must buy new kits | The cup will be given to the team next season | *Losing may make it harder to win next season | Next season has been cancelled
exam: In the sentence «Her silence had serious implications for the plan.», what does implication mean? | *A result or effect that will come later | A loud noise | Something that has no effect at all | A small picture
